Q: UI Freezing when passing prop function from parent to child component A: <p>Your issue is caused by parent rerendering and in turn, child component rerenders too. And those rerenders happen in your code because <code>message</code> state updates from children happen on every click.</p>
<p>In order to prevent rerendering children component, you need to memoiza the component, as it updates parent state, but child component does not changes due to that. So we can use <code>React.memo</code>.</p>
<p>But now we need to also &quot;stabilize&quot; the prop passed to children component. This can be done with <code>useCallback</code> hook.</p>
<p>In order to fix this you should:</p>
<ul>
<li>Memoize the callback in the parent using <code>useCallback</code></li>
<li>Memoize the child component using <code>React.memo</code></li>
</ul>
<p>Below is simplified setup of your components:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const Support = () =&gt; {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState(null);

  const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
  }, []);

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;h1&gt;{message} : Data from Child&lt;/h1&gt;
      &lt;GridData handleChildData={handleChildData} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
};

const GridData = React.memo(({ handleChildData }) =&gt; {
  const table = useMaterialReactTable(
    useMemo(() =&gt; ({
      data,
      columns,
      muiTableBodyRowProps: ({ row }) =&gt; ({
        onClick: () =&gt; handleChildData(row.original.name),
        sx: { cursor: 'pointer' },
      }),
    }), [data, columns, handleChildData])
  );

  return &lt;MaterialReactTable table={table} /&gt;;
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>BONUS</strong></p>
<p>Setters returned from <code>useState</code> hook are stable, so you can pass <code>setMesage</code> function and omit entirely</p>
<pre><code>const handleChildData = useCallback((data) =&gt; {
    setMessage(data);
}, []);
</code></pre>
<p>and then pass <code>setMessage</code> directly</p>
<pre><code>&lt;GridData handleChildData={setMessage} /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>It will keep working the same :)</p>

Q: Get all css root variables in array using javascript and change the values A: <p>Based on the other answers...</p>
<p>Here's some code to get <code>:root</code> variables and their values as an object.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>export type Theme = Record&lt;`--${string}`, string&gt;;

export const getTheme = (): Theme =&gt; {
  const rootStyles = window.getComputedStyle(document.documentElement);
  return Object.fromEntries(
    Array.from(document.styleSheets)
      .flatMap((styleSheet) =&gt; {
        try {
          return Array.from(styleSheet.cssRules);
        } catch (error) {
          return [];
        }
      })
      .filter((cssRule) =&gt; cssRule instanceof CSSStyleRule)
      .flatMap((cssRule) =&gt; Array.from(cssRule.style))
      .filter((style) =&gt; style.startsWith(&quot;--&quot;))
      .map((variable) =&gt; [variable, rootStyles.getPropertyValue(variable)]),
  );
};
</code></pre>
<p>Adds catching access exceptions, e.g. Google Font stylesheets (<code>Uncaught DOMException: Failed to read the 'cssRules' property from 'CSSStyleSheet': Cannot access rules</code>).</p>
<p>Example results:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;--accent&quot;: &quot;hsl(342, 50%, 50%)&quot;,
  &quot;--dark-gray&quot;: &quot;#606060&quot;,
  &quot;--gray&quot;: &quot;#b0b0b0&quot;,
  &quot;--light-gray&quot;: &quot;#e0e0e0&quot;,
  &quot;--success&quot;: &quot;#10b981&quot;,
  &quot;--warning&quot;: &quot;#f59e0b&quot;,
  &quot;--error&quot;: &quot;#f43f5e&quot;,
  &quot;--sans&quot;: &quot;\&quot;Poppins\&quot;, sans-serif&quot;,
  &quot;--mono&quot;: &quot;\&quot;IBM Plex Mono\&quot;, monospace&quot;,
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Or, if you don't care about the order of the results matching the order you defined in the stylesheet:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>export const getTheme = (): Theme =&gt; {
  const styles = window.getComputedStyle(document.documentElement);
  const vars = Object.values(
    window.getComputedStyle(document.documentElement),
  ).filter((value) =&gt; value.startsWith(&quot;--&quot;));
  return Object.fromEntries(
    vars.map((key) =&gt; [key, styles.getPropertyValue(key).trim()]),
  );
};
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Note: If you already know the variable name and just want to get it, it's sufficient to do <code>getComputedStyle(someElement).getPropertyValue(--some-css-var)</code>. Unfortunately <code>getComputedStyle</code> doesn't provide any way to iterate over variable properties, only static properties e.g. <code>display</code>.</p>
<hr />
<p>If you want this to be reactive, just re-run the func whenever you know these variables will change. A React hook example:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>export const useTheme = () =&gt; {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState&lt;Theme&gt;({});

  // update reactive state, trigger re-render
  const update = useCallback(() =&gt; setTheme(getTheme()), []);

  // when some kind of reactive dark mode variable changes, e.g. a Jotai atom
  useEffect(() =&gt; { update(); }, [isDarkMode]);

  // or when e.g. &lt;html data-dark&gt; changes
  useMutationObserver(update, document.documentElement, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: [&quot;data-dark&quot;] });

  // or if you have an e.g. --font-sans google font, update when fonts done loading
  useEventListener(&quot;loadingdone&quot;, update, document.fonts);

  return theme;
};
</code></pre>
